Criticize the quoted WORKING ANSWER against the PROBLEM. Each objection must locate the EARLIEST public derivation step where the error enters, bind its fork index and assign a stable nonempty participant branch label, and explain why it is the first defective step, state the alleged defect, and state what the objection would defeat. Do not require a concrete check in this condition. Do not repeat a resolved objection without identifying a concrete failure in its disposition. Do not request generic caution or manufacture an objection. Limit working to 6000 characters and objections to at most three. Return an empty objections list when none survives. If you cannot determine whether any objection survives because a particular derivation is missing, return cannot_decide and name it. Schema: prose-objection-r3-a2.schema.json. For each fork, either set step_quote to the empty string and give the exact step_index, or quote at least 40 characters from that step. Reflowed whitespace, case, and straight/curly quote forms are accepted after normalization. A nonempty fabricated or paraphrased quote is invalid even when step_index is valid.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "BEGIN PROBLEM (504 UTF-8 bytes; sha256=c10a8226769f2dac9cbfea423c0f9a45f2c114e8060aaf978d649e1896e657fe)
A fictional town is establishing a shared workshop with a small number of machines and many residents who want to use them. It wants newcomers to get a fair chance, experienced users to complete demanding projects, few unused bookings, and rules that residents can understand and challenge. The town does not know how demand or strategic booking will change once the rules are introduced. Design a workable allocation arrangement and explain why it should remain fair and useful as people respond to it.

